<h2>Intermediate — 11 Ways to Turn Sessions into a System</h2>

<p>The <a href="#!tip-beginner">beginner part</a> was about keeping one session clean; the intermediate part is about <strong>moving whatever repeats into the system</strong>. Run research in parallel, freeze a good prompt into a skill, use CLAUDE.md as a router — tips 10 through 20 of the series, checked against the official docs.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">About this document (sources)</span>
    · Grounded in the <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official Claude Code docs</a> (as of 2026-08-14)
</div>

<h3>10. Run subagents in parallel</h3>
<p>A subagent is a separate agent your main session spawns like a deputy. Each one gets <strong>its own context window</strong> and reports only a summary back, which makes them perfect for read-heavy work like research and verification — whatever they read never touches your main context. And since they run in parallel, you save wall-clock time too.</p>
<pre><code>I want to research what matters most in Instagram marketing
for a beauty brand. Run parallel subagents and gather 10+ references —
one agent on success stories, one on failures, one on recent algorithm
changes — then synthesize the common threads for me at the end.</code></pre>

