<p>This wiki runs on the same pattern, by the way. The <strong>multi-source cross-verification</strong> done before registering a news article — reading several outlets covering the same event at once and checking that figures and dates agree — is exactly this (the fact-check notes at the bottom of each article are its product). Same for any mountain-of-reading job like a <strong>style audit across a hundred-plus documents</strong>: subagents split the reading, and the main session receives summaries and only judges.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run agents in parallel</a> · <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Create custom subagents</a>. Subagents never talk to each other — when they need to, that's the <a href="#!tip-advanced">advanced part</a>'s agent teams.</p>

<h3>11. Build your own skills</h3>
<p>A skill is a <strong>reusable prompt file</strong> living under <code>.claude/skills/</code>. Define a long instruction once instead of pasting it every time, and Claude pulls it out whenever a request matches its description — or you invoke it directly with a slash. Keep asking for the same thing over and over? That's your signal to make a skill. And making one couldn't be easier: say <strong>"turn what we just did into a skill"</strong> and Claude writes it for you. Devices like this, built for predictable results, are collectively called a harness — and skills are the star example.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">This wiki's <a href="#!skl-overview">skill course</a> covers the topic in 3 chapters, from principles to security. Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Skills</a></p>

<h3>12. Use a cheap model for subagents</h3>
<p>Searching and fetching text doesn't need a frontier model — that's just waste. When delegating, add one phrase: <strong>"run the subagents on Haiku."</strong> Or pin it with the <code>model: haiku</code> field in a custom subagent definition. Cheap fast models for collection, expensive ones for synthesis and design judgment — that split saves serious tokens.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Per-model pricing: <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official pricing docs</a> — as of this writing, Haiku 4.5 runs $1 in / $5 out per million tokens, a fraction of the top models.</p>

<h3>13. Update CLAUDE.md when a session ends — capped at 150–200 lines</h3>
<p>New convention this session? Way of working changed? Put it in CLAUDE.md, or the Claude in your next session won't know — it has no context. One caution though: CLAUDE.md is <strong>read unconditionally at every session start</strong>, so the longer it grows the more startup context it eats. When you ask for the update, always add "keep it under 150–200 lines" — Claude compresses it nicely. The official docs agree: if you can't skim it between meetings, it's too long.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Memory — CLAUDE.md</a></p>

<h3>14. Use CLAUDE.md as a router</h3>
<p>There's a way to get both the cap from tip 13 and the "but I want to hand over lots of info" itch. Instead of putting content into CLAUDE.md, put in <strong>a pointer: "if you need X, read this file."</strong> Claude visits that file only when the need arises — full context delivered, standing cost saved. A clever little structure.</p>
<pre><code>I've written up this project's code structure in docs/architecture.md.
Add a router entry to CLAUDE.md telling any command or session curious
about the code structure to read that file. Just one line in CLAUDE.md itself.</code></pre>

<h3>15. When something looks wrong, interrupt — don't wait</h3>
<p>See the direction going wrong mid-run? <strong>Stop it right away</strong> (Esc). There's zero reason to fear the stop button. Then ask "my intent was X — why did you do it that way?" and Claude explains its reasoning; correct the course and let it continue. Waiting until the end wastes time and tokens, and worse — <strong>a wrong result sitting in the context contaminates every judgment after it</strong>.</p>

<h3>16. Push back — and when it lands, record it</h3>
<p>First result disappointing, but you can't put your finger on the fix? Push back anyway, even vaguely. Claude now knows the first attempt missed, so the second try lands far more often. Most people try once, shrug "guess it can't do it," and give up — push two or three times and you may be genuinely surprised. And <strong>the moment you get what you wanted is the moment to record it</strong> — walk away without recording and you'll retrace the whole path next time.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># When it's not right — you don't need to know the fix</span>
Not quite. Make it more refined, raise the polish a notch, and try again.
Also give me one alternative from a completely different angle.

<span class="c"># When it lands — don't stop here</span>
This result is great. Turn what we just did into a skill,
so next time "card-news style" gets me exactly this.</code></pre>

<h3>17. Rewind time with <code>/rewind</code></h3>
<p>When a mid-task result goes wrong, asking in prose to "please undo that" can introduce new errors during the undoing. Use <code>/rewind</code> instead — pick a point from the checkpoint list and <strong>every file edit after it rolls back wholesale</strong>. You can choose to restore the code, the conversation, or both.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/checkpointing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Checkpointing</a> — edits accumulate as checkpoints automatically during a session.</p>

<h3>18. Get notified with hooks</h3>
<p>A hook is a command that fires at specific moments in Claude Code's lifecycle — task finished, approval needed, and so on. Say "set up a hook that plays a sound whenever a session finishes" and Claude writes it into your settings. With one session you won't miss it; once you're running ten sessions across several projects, a device that tells you <strong>what finished where</strong> becomes essential. The minimal version: set <code>preferredNotifChannel</code> to <code>terminal_bell</code>.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks-guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Automate actions with hooks</a> — see also this wiki's the <a href="#!skills">extending chapter (Hooks section)</a>.</p>

<h3>19. Claude has eyes — hand it images</h3>
<p>Error messages, a website you love, a UI screen — anything hard to put into words, <strong>screenshot it and paste it in</strong>. Both the terminal and the desktop app take dragged or pasted images, and Claude reads them. Tossing in a captured error screen beats transcribing the error text by hand, on both speed and accuracy.</p>

<h3>20. Turn screenshots into templates</h3>
<p>An application of tip 19. Capture a design you'd love as a reference — a card-news layout, a page with great color — hand it over and say it explicitly: <strong>"make a template based on this image."</strong> The point isn't copying it verbatim; it's extracting the color and layout structure into something you can reuse.</p>

